# ABSTRACT

A Redundant Array of Independent Disks (RAID) decoding system incorporating a built-in self-test (BIST) mechanism is disclosed. The system comprises a Reed-Solomon decoder configured to receive data and parity information distributed across a plurality of storage devices, and to compute one or more syndrome values indicative of error conditions within a codeword. A BIST controller is coupled to the decoder and is configured to selectively inject test patterns or utilize existing parity data to generate expected syndrome values, which are compared against syndrome values produced during normal decoding operations. A comparator circuit evaluates the computed syndromes against the expected reference values to determine whether the decoding logic, parity generation circuitry, and associated data paths are functioning within acceptable operational parameters. Upon detection of a discrepancy exceeding a predetermined threshold, the system generates a fault indication signal identifying a potential defect in the RAID controller, decoder logic, or storage medium, without requiring removal of the array from service. The self-test may be executed periodically, upon system initialization, or in response to a triggering event, and may operate using a subset of available parity symbols to minimize impact on ongoing input/output operations. The disclosed system thereby enables in-situ verification of Reed-Solomon-based error correction integrity, improving reliability, reducing undetected silent data corruption, and facilitating proactive maintenance in RAID storage architectures.

---

**Claim (independent, exemplary):**

1. A RAID decoding system comprising:
a plurality of storage devices configured to store data symbols and corresponding Reed-Solomon parity symbols;
a Reed-Solomon decoder configured to compute at least one syndrome value from the data symbols and parity symbols;
a built-in self-test (BIST) controller configured to generate an expected syndrome value using at least a portion of the parity symbols;
a comparator configured to compare the computed syndrome value to the expected syndrome value; and
a fault detection module configured to generate a fault signal when a difference between the computed syndrome value and the expected syndrome value exceeds a predetermined threshold, wherein the BIST controller performs the comparison without interrupting active read or write operations of the RAID system.
